@page "/"
@implements IDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject NotificationService Notifications
@inject TripRequestStore Store
@inject GetawaysAppSettings Settings

@* 2026-10-28 (Baaria): the agency's pages are named in its own voice (Razor Sharp Trip Requests) and the window title is the
   agency's name. 2026-10-29 (Baaria): the Sales (charts) and Front Desk (ribbon) pages were removed; the Dashboard carries a chart. *@

@* 2026-10-25 (Baaria, SC-344): the hero grid is a travel agency's quote desk, Inbox — Trip Requests. It replaced an example whose
   banner text, columns and names came from an earlier custom-quote inbox. Same grid features as before (1,000 windowed rows, frozen
   key column, in-cell DropDownGridControl lookup, fast-typing money cell, SVG sparkline, badges, popup editor); two buttons that only
   raised a notification now do their job: Export to Excel calls the grid's ExportToXlsxAsync, New Request opens the editor on a blank
   request and adds it on Save. The "grid" string in the code tab is the GridControl block below, copied verbatim (scratchpad
   home_inbox.py regenerates it). *@

@* 2026-10-25 (Baaria: "not very interactive"): the dropdown, Edit button and header sort all worked, but on the live site each
   answered in 0.4 to 1.6 s with no sign of life, and a second click undid the first (closed the list, flipped the sort). The grid
   rendered about 144 rows (8 columns, so the default 60-row overscan per side), each with its own DropDownGridControl; WindowOverscanRows
   is now 15, about 42 rows. Trip type, Fare trend and Itinerary had no Field, so their headers could not sort; they now sort by
   TripType, FareDeltaPercent and Itinerary. Headers lost their "(DropDownGrid)" style suffixes so the sort arrow fits; the feature
   cards above the grid name the controls. Rows get an opaque background in app.css: frozen cells use background-color: inherit,
   and a row with no background let the scrolled cells show through the Request column. *@

<PageTitle>FlexCore: the Blazor toolbox that gets data right.</PageTitle>

<div class="landing-container">
    @* 1. Hero *@
    <div class="top-hero-compact">
        @* 2026-10-28 (Baaria): hero rewritten. Each claim is checked: 100,000 rows on /demo/grid/row-selection-50k, Crystal import
           via CrystalXmlReportLoader (XML exports), 137 standalone controls counted in the launch audit, both packages MIT.
           The pill cycles grids, charts, reports in CSS (Home.razor.css, "word pill"); screen readers get the plain sentence.
           Headline chosen by Baaria 2026-10-28 from the toolbox ideas. *@
        <h1 class="hero-oneliner">
            <span class="visually-hidden">The Blazor toolbox that does grids, charts and reports right.</span>
            <span aria-hidden="true">The Blazor toolbox <br class="hero-br-lg" />that does <br class="hero-br-sm" /><span class="text-nowrap"><span class="word-pill"><span class="word-pill-mark"></span><span class="word-pill-words"><span>grids</span><span>charts</span><span>reports</span></span></span> right.</span></span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">
            130+ Blazor controls for data-heavy apps.<br class="hero-br-lg" />
            Written in C#, ready to drop straight into your project.
        </p>
        @* Two calls to action under the hero (2026-10-28, after notion.com). Plain links, not ButtonControl: it renders a <button>
           with no Href, and these must stay real links (new tab, crawlers). "Quick Start" matches the top-bar button. "Documentation"
           opens the component catalog: each control's page carries its parameter tables and code, and there is no separate docs site. *@
        <div class="hero-actions">
            <a href="quick-start" class="hero-btn hero-btn-primary">Quick Start</a>
            <a href="components" class="hero-btn hero-btn-secondary">Documentation</a>
        </div>
    </div>


    @* 2. The app window (2026-10-29, Baaria, after the app demo on blazor.radzen.com): Razor Sharp Getaways as a working app. Since
       2026-11-04 (Baaria) the window is also the Quick Start project, quickstart/RazorSharpGetaways: its pages, window parts, data
       and stylesheet are that project's files, compiled into this site from there (FlexCore.Showcase.csproj), so the download and the
       demo cannot drift. This file keeps only what is the site's: the frame, the Example | Razor | C# tabs over the window, and the
       feature strip above the grid. Pages are switched here, not routed; ?tab= names one (the old keys grid, bookings and reports
       still work). *@
    <section id="live-showcase" class="showcase-section">
        <div class="showcase-shell">
            @* Window title bar *@
            <div class="window-titlebar">
                <div class="window-controls">
                    <span class="window-dot dot-red"></span>
                    <span class="window-dot dot-yellow"></span>
                    <span class="window-dot dot-green"></span>
                </div>
                <div class="window-title">
                    <span class="bi bi-window-sidebar"></span>
                    <span>@GetawaysPages.AppName</span>
                </div>
                <div class="window-tags">
                    <TabStripControl Items="_viewTabs" SelectedIndex="_codeTab" SelectedIndexChanged="SetCodeTab" OnTabClose="@(_ => SetCodeTab(0))"
                                     CssClass="hero-view-tabs site-tabs" Orientation="TabOrientation.Horizontal" Theme="TabTheme.Minimal" ShowOrientationMenu="false" />
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="@($"gw-app {Settings.SchemeClass} {(Settings.SidebarIcons ? "gw-nav-collapsed" : "")} {(_navOpen ? "gw-nav-open" : "")}")" style="@Settings.WindowStyle">
                <GetawaysSidebar SelectedIndex="@GetawaysPages.IndexOf(_page)" OnSelect="@(i => Go(GetawaysPages.All[i].Key))" />
                @if (_navOpen)
                {
                    <div class="gw-scrim" @onclick="@(() => _navOpen = false)"></div>
                }

                <div class="gw-main">
                    <GetawaysAppBar Title="@CurrentPage.Title" OnMenuClick="ToggleMenu" OnOpenRequest="OpenRequest" OnShowNewRequests="@(() => ShowRequests("New requests"))" />

                    <div class="gw-page">
                        @* 2026-10-24 (Baaria): the Razor code is a tab over the example, not a drawer under it. The live page stays
                           mounted while hidden, so the grid keeps its state when the reader switches back. *@
                        @if (_codeTab > 0)
                        {
                            <div class="code-drawer">
                                <div class="code-header">
                                    <span>@(_codeTab == 2 ? "C#" : "Razor") for the @CurrentPage.Nav page, quickstart/RazorSharpGetaways</span>
                                    <ButtonControl BareStyle="true" CssClass="btn-copy-code" OnClick="CopyActiveCode">@(_copiedCode ? "✓ Copied!" : "📋 Copy Code")</ButtonControl>
                                </div>
                                <pre><code>@GetActiveCodeSnippet()</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        }
                        <div class="@(_codeTab > 0 ? "showcase-live is-hidden" : "showcase-live")">
                            @switch (_page)
                            {
                                case "requests":
                                    <GetawaysRequests OpenRequestId="_openRequestId" StartNew="_startNew">
                                        <AboveGrid>
                                            @* The site's feature strip; the download's page has nothing here *@
                                            <div class="grid-perf-highlights">
                                                <div class="perf-badge-item">
                                                    <span class="perf-badge-icon">⚡</span>
                                                    <div class="perf-badge-text">
                                                        <strong>1,000 Rows Live Virtualized</strong>
                                                        <span>Only the rows in view plus an overscan band are rendered as you scroll</span>
                                                    </div>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="perf-badge-item">
                                                    <span class="perf-badge-icon">⌨️</span>
                                                    <div class="perf-badge-text">
                                                        <strong>Instant In-Grid Typing</strong>
                                                        <span>Click any Quote cell to type directly — keystrokes stay in the browser until commit</span>
                                                    </div>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="perf-badge-item">
                                                    <span class="perf-badge-icon">🎛️</span>
                                                    <div class="perf-badge-text">
                                                        <strong>DropDownGridControl</strong>
                                                        <span>Multi-column trip-type picker with search &amp; sort</span>
                                                    </div>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="perf-badge-item">
                                                    <span class="perf-badge-icon">✏️</span>
                                                    <div class="perf-badge-text">
                                                        <strong>DialogControl Editor</strong>
                                                        <span>Click [✏ Edit] on Itinerary for the full request editor</span>
                                                    </div>
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                        </AboveGrid>
                                    </GetawaysRequests>
                                    break;
                                case "itinerary":
                                    <GetawaysBookings />
                                    break;
                                case "invoices":
                                    <GetawaysInvoices />
                                    break;
                                case "settings":
                                    <GetawaysSettings OnOpenDashboard="@(() => Go("dashboard"))" />
                                    break;
                                default:
                                    <GetawaysDashboard OnShowRequests="ShowRequests" OnOpenRequest="OpenRequest" />
                                    break;
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    @* The + button: create a request, or jump to the page that makes a booking or an invoice *@
                    <GetawaysFab OnNewRequest="NewRequest" OnItineraryEntry="ItineraryEntry" OnInvoice="@(() => Go("invoices"))" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    @* Four-colour divider between home sections (2026-10-29, Baaria), the bar that sits under each page title *@
    <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    @* ── 3. The four big workhorses (2026-10-28, Baaria). They replace the six small workhorse cards on this page; the components
       page keeps those. Every line is checked: 100,000 rows (/demo/grid/row-selection-50k); 42 values in ChartType;
       CrystalXmlReportLoader, DrillDownFilter and ReportDesignerCanvasControl in FlexCore/Reports; SchedulerView has Day, Week, Month,
       Timeline and Agenda, and Gantt is its own control. The pictures are plain SVG, decoration only, in the logo's four colours.
       The heading is a plain section heading with a hand-drawn chequered flag beside it (it replaced a dark finish-line ribbon that
       read as cartoonish); the flag waves each time the heading scrolls into view: fxToggleClassWhenVisible (App.razor) adds
       is-playing and Home.razor.css does the rest. The picture sits left of the heading text, like the crane and the scale below. *@
    <section class="workhorses-section">
        <div class="doodle-head workhorses-head" @ref="_workhorsesHead">
            <svg class="doodle finish-flag" viewBox="0 0 170 150" aria-hidden="true">
                <g class="flag-sway">
                    <path d="M37 142 C38 110 39 62 40 16" fill="none" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round" />
                    <circle cx="40" cy="12" r="5" fill="#0f172a" />
                    @for (var col = 0; col < FlagColumns; col++)
                    {
                        var c = col;
                        <g class="flag-col" style="@($"--d: {c * 90}ms; --a: {1 + c * 2}px")">
                            @foreach (var cell in _flagCells.Where(f => f.Col == c))
                            {
                                <polygon points="@cell.Points" fill="@(cell.Dark ? "#0f172a" : "#ffffff")" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                            }
                        </g>
                    }
                </g>
                <g class="flag-wind" fill="none" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round">
                    <path d="M157 34 q5 -3 10 0" />
                    <path d="M155 54 q6 -3 12 0" />
                    <path d="M158 74 q4 -2 8 0" />
                </g>
                <path d="M17 143 q10 -4 20 0 t20 0" fill="none" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" />
            </svg>
            <h2 class="section-title">Everything you need to cross the finish line</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="workhorse-grid">
            <a href="demo/grid" class="workhorse-card">
                <div class="workhorse-art blue" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 240 120" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
                        <rect x="20" y="14" width="200" height="16" rx="4" fill="#0284c7" opacity="0.85" />
                        @for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++)
                        {
                            <rect x="20" y="@(36 + i * 15)" width="200" height="11" rx="3" fill="@(i == 2 ? "#7dd3fc" : "#ffffff")" />
                            <rect x="26" y="@(39 + i * 15)" width="34" height="5" rx="2" fill="#38bdf8" opacity="0.7" />
                            <rect x="72" y="@(39 + i * 15)" width="58" height="5" rx="2" fill="#cbd5e1" />
                            <rect x="150" y="@(39 + i * 15)" width="36" height="5" rx="2" fill="#cbd5e1" />
                        }
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div class="workhorse-body">
                    <h3 class="workhorse-name"><span class="workhorse-tile blue"><span class="bi bi-table"></span></span> Data Grid</h3>
                    <p class="workhorse-desc">Scroll 100,000 rows, type straight into cells, then export the lot to Excel.</p>
                    <ul class="workhorse-chips">
                        <li>Virtual scrolling</li><li>Frozen columns</li><li>Tree grid</li><li>Excel export</li>
                    </ul>
                    <span class="workhorse-link">Explore the grid &rarr;</span>
                </div>
            </a>

            <a href="demo/charts" class="workhorse-card">
                <div class="workhorse-art indigo" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 240 120" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
                        <line x1="24" y1="104" x2="216" y2="104" stroke="#c7d2fe" stroke-width="2" />
                        @{ var bars = new[] { 38, 56, 44, 70, 62, 84 }; }
                        @for (var i = 0; i < bars.Length; i++)
                        {
                            <rect x="@(34 + i * 30)" y="@(104 - bars[i])" width="18" height="@bars[i]" rx="3" fill="#818cf8" opacity="0.75" />
                        }
                        <polyline points="43,58 73,40 103,50 133,26 163,32 193,12" fill="none" stroke="#4338ca" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div class="workhorse-body">
                    <h3 class="workhorse-name"><span class="workhorse-tile indigo"><span class="bi bi-pie-chart-fill"></span></span> Charts</h3>
                    <p class="workhorse-desc">35+ chart types drawn in SVG from C#, from sparklines and gauges to Sankey diagrams.</p>
                    <ul class="workhorse-chips">
                        <li>Bar &amp; line</li><li>Pie &amp; donut</li><li>Gauges</li><li>Financial</li>
                    </ul>
                    <span class="workhorse-link">Explore the charts &rarr;</span>
                </div>
            </a>

            <a href="demo/reports/package-costs" class="workhorse-card">
                <div class="workhorse-art green" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 240 120" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
                        <rect x="70" y="8" width="100" height="108" rx="6" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#6ee7b7" stroke-width="2" />
                        <rect x="82" y="20" width="52" height="7" rx="2" fill="#059669" />
                        @for (var i = 0; i < 4; i++)
                        {
                            <rect x="82" y="@(40 + i * 14)" width="44" height="5" rx="2" fill="#cbd5e1" />
                            <rect x="136" y="@(40 + i * 14)" width="22" height="5" rx="2" fill="#6ee7b7" />
                        }
                        <line x1="82" y1="98" x2="158" y2="98" stroke="#059669" stroke-width="2" />
                        <rect x="130" y="103" width="28" height="6" rx="2" fill="#059669" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div class="workhorse-body">
                    <h3 class="workhorse-name"><span class="workhorse-tile green"><span class="bi bi-file-earmark-richtext"></span></span> Reports</h3>
                    <p class="workhorse-desc">Bring your Crystal Reports across and run them in the browser, with parameters and drill-down.</p>
                    <ul class="workhorse-chips">
                        <li>Crystal import</li><li>Parameters</li><li>Drill-down</li><li>Report designer</li>
                    </ul>
                    <span class="workhorse-link">Explore the reports &rarr;</span>
                </div>
            </a>

            <a href="demo/scheduler" class="workhorse-card">
                <div class="workhorse-art sky" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 240 120" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
                        <rect x="20" y="12" width="200" height="14" rx="4" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.85" />
                        @for (var c = 0; c < 7; c++)
                        {
                            <line x1="@(20 + c * 200 / 7)" y1="30" x2="@(20 + c * 200 / 7)" y2="110" stroke="#bae6fd" stroke-width="1" />
                        }
                        <rect x="24" y="36" width="50" height="14" rx="3" fill="#38bdf8" />
                        <rect x="82" y="56" width="78" height="14" rx="3" fill="#7dd3fc" />
                        <rect x="110" y="34" width="44" height="14" rx="3" fill="#0369a1" opacity="0.8" />
                        <rect x="168" y="76" width="46" height="14" rx="3" fill="#38bdf8" />
                        <rect x="40" y="90" width="64" height="14" rx="3" fill="#bae6fd" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div class="workhorse-body">
                    <h3 class="workhorse-name"><span class="workhorse-tile sky"><span class="bi bi-calendar-week"></span></span> Scheduler</h3>
                    <p class="workhorse-desc">Day, week, month, timeline and agenda views, plus a Gantt chart for the long jobs.</p>
                    <ul class="workhorse-chips">
                        <li>Day &amp; week</li><li>Month</li><li>Timeline</li><li>Gantt</li>
                    </ul>
                    <span class="workhorse-link">Explore the scheduler &rarr;</span>
                </div>
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ── 4. The component directory ── *@
    @* Four-colour divider between home sections (2026-10-29, Baaria), the bar that sits under each page title *@
    <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <ComponentCatalogDirectory ShowWorkhorses="false" />


    @* 5. Pillars, on the page's one coloured band (2026-10-28, Baaria, after the stats bar and blue band on telerik.com): a green
       strip of numbers straddles the top of a full-width indigo band, both colours from the logo. The numbers are checked: 137
       standalone controls (launch audit), 42 ChartType values and 100,000 rows on /demo/grid/row-selection-50k. Three since
       2026-10-29: Baaria dropped the fourth (first "0 npm packages", then "MIT licensed on NuGet"). *@
    <div class="stats-strip">
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">130+</span><span class="stat-label">Blazor controls</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">35+</span><span class="stat-label">chart types</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-num">100K</span><span class="stat-label">rows in one grid</span></div>
    </div>
    <section class="pillars-section brand-band">
        <div class="section-header">
            <div class="doodle-head" @ref="_pillarsHead">
                @* Crane in place of the 🏗️ emoji (2026-10-28): white ink for the indigo band. It lowers the FlexCore logo to the ground and
                   hoists it back with a swing when the heading scrolls into view. The cable and load are clipped at the jib so the cable
                   never shows above it. *@
                <svg class="doodle crane-doodle" viewBox="0 0 170 150" aria-hidden="true">
                    <defs><clipPath id="crane-below-jib"><rect x="0" y="31" width="170" height="125" /></clipPath></defs>
                    <g fill="none" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                        <path d="M40 140 V30 M52 140 V30" stroke-width="3" />
                        <path d="M40 140 L52 122 L40 104 L52 86 L40 68 L52 50 L40 32" stroke-width="2" />
                        <path d="M22 30 H156" stroke-width="3" />
                        <path d="M46 30 V10 M46 10 L22 30 M46 10 L156 30" stroke-width="2" />
                        <path d="M20 141 q12 -4 24 0 t24 0 M98 141 q12 -4 22 0 t22 0" stroke-width="2.5" />
                    </g>
                    <rect x="22" y="31" width="12" height="10" rx="2" fill="#ffffff" />
                    <rect x="113" y="27" width="14" height="7" rx="2" fill="#ffffff" />
                    <g clip-path="url(#crane-below-jib)">
                        <g class="cr-load">
                            <path d="M120 0 V62" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2" />
                            <path d="M120 62 v5 a4 4 0 1 1 -4 4 M120 70 L108 74 M120 70 L132 74" fill="none" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" />
                            <rect x="106" y="74" width="28" height="28" rx="5" fill="#ffffff" />
                            <rect x="109" y="77" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#0284c7" />
                            <rect x="121" y="77" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#38bdf8" />
                            <rect x="109" y="89" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#6366f1" />
                            <rect x="121" y="89" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#10b981" />
                        </g>
                    </g>
                </svg>
                <h2 class="section-title">Engineered for Enterprise Growth</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="section-desc">
                The desktop-app power business users expect, rebuilt for the browser in Blazor.
            </p>
        </div>

        @* 2026-11-04 (Baaria): card text rewritten to say what each one saves a team. Each line checks out: 100,000 rows on
           /demo/grid/row-selection-50k; MdiHost, GridTheme.Vb6Windows, TreeViewControl.UseVb6TreeStyle and CrystalXmlReportLoader, with no
           Crystal package in FlexCore.csproj; AggregateRows and ExportToXlsxAsync; IReportDataExecutor, IReportSessionContext, IGridSettingsStore. *@
        <div class="pillars-grid">
            <div class="pillar-card">
                <div class="pillar-icon blue">⚡</div>
                <h3 class="pillar-title">Ultra-Fast Virtualization</h3>
                <p class="pillar-desc">
                    Big datasets stop being a performance project. Scroll 100,000 rows while the grid renders only a small window of them.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pillar-card">
                <div class="pillar-icon purple">🛡️</div>
                <h3 class="pillar-title">No npm, No Bundler</h3>
                <p class="pillar-desc">
                    No node_modules to audit and no JavaScript build to maintain. Add the package and your team stays in C#.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pillar-card">
                <div class="pillar-icon green">🏢</div>
                <h3 class="pillar-title">Legacy VB / WinForms Migration</h3>
                <p class="pillar-desc">
                    Move to the web and keep the screens users already know: MDI windows, VB6-style grids and trees, and Crystal Reports XML exports with no Crystal runtime.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pillar-card">
                <div class="pillar-icon amber">📊</div>
                <h3 class="pillar-title">Excel-Grade Grid Power</h3>
                <p class="pillar-desc">
                    The spreadsheet features users demand, already built: frozen columns, tree grids, totals, in-cell editing, and Excel export.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pillar-card">
                <div class="pillar-icon rose">🎨</div>
                <h3 class="pillar-title">Adaptive Layout &amp; MDI</h3>
                <p class="pillar-desc">
                    Stop hand-building app shells. Collapsible panels, splitters, tab strips, and multi-document windows are ready to drop in.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pillar-card">
                <div class="pillar-icon cyan">🔌</div>
                <h3 class="pillar-title">Pluggable &amp; Host-Agnostic</h3>
                <p class="pillar-desc">
                    Keep your stack. Report data, session context, and saved grid settings plug in through C# interfaces, so EF Core, Dapper, or raw SQL all fit.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* 6. Comparison table *@
    <section class="comparison-section">
        <div class="section-header">
            <div class="doodle-head" @ref="_compareHead">
                @* Balance scale in place of the ⚖️ emoji (2026-10-28): the beam rocks when the heading scrolls into view and settles with
                   the FlexCore logo's pan low and the feather's pan high. The pans stay level: they move up and down with the beam ends
                   (Home.razor.css works out each pan's offset from the beam angle). *@
                <svg class="doodle scale-doodle" viewBox="0 0 170 150" aria-hidden="true">
                    <path d="M85 128 V34" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" />
                    <path d="M62 140 L108 140 L99 128 L71 128 Z" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                    <circle cx="85" cy="30" r="5" fill="#0f172a" />
                    <g class="sc-beam"><path d="M30 34 H140" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round" /></g>
                    <g class="sc-pan sc-left">
                        <path d="M30 34 L16 82 M30 34 L44 82" fill="none" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="2" />
                        <rect x="20" y="61" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#0284c7" />
                        <rect x="31" y="61" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#38bdf8" />
                        <rect x="20" y="72" width="10" height="10" rx="2" fill="#6366f1" />
